# Replicating methods

## Dog impulsivity

:::{.fragment}
![](media/dog_inhibition_chasing_pete-godfrey.png){.absolute top="8%" left="14%" height="300" fig-alt="Brown dog chasing cormorant on the beach."}

![](media/dog_inhibition_rolling_michael-oxendine.png){.absolute top="51%" left="0%" height="300" fig-alt="Brown dog rolling around on grass."}

![](media/dog_patience2_tamas-pap.png){.absolute top="51%" left="53%" height="300" fig-alt="Brown dog licking treats in a jar."}
:::


## Owner perceptions of dog impulsivity

::: {.callout-tip}
## Research Question

<!-- ::: {.fragment} -->

::: {style="font-size: 40px"}
Do owner perceptions of dog impulsivity match impulsivity in a behavioral task?
:::
<!-- ::: -->
:::

::: {.fragment}
Based on and replicated [Brady et al., 2018](https://doi.org/10.1016/j.applanim.2018.01.003)
:::

::: {.source}
[Stevens et al. 2022, _Animal Behavior & Cognition_](https://doi.org/10.26451/abc.09.03.02.2022)
:::


## Measuring owner perceptions

### Dog Impulsivity Assessment Scale (DIAS)

::: {.fragment}
 * I would consider my dog to be very impulsive
 * My dog does not think before it acts
 * My dog appears to have a lot of control over how it responds
 * My dog is not very patient

[Wright et al., 2011](https://doi.org/10.46867/ijcp.2011.24.02.03)

:::

::: {.source}
[Stevens et al. 2022, _Animal Behavior & Cognition_](https://doi.org/10.26451/abc.09.03.02.2022)
:::


## Measuring spatial impulsivity

::: {.r-stack}
![](media/dog_spatial_apparatus.jpg){height="490" fig-align="center" fig-align="center" fig-alt="Brown dog looking at plate of three treats next to plate of one treat."}

![](media/dog_spatial_setup.png){.fragment height="580" fig-align="center" fig-alt="Illustration of experimental set-up with dog and handler at one end near the curtain, with plates of treats on other side of curtain and experimenter at far end."}
:::

::: {.source}
[Stevens et al. 2022, _Animal Behavior & Cognition_](https://doi.org/10.26451/abc.09.03.02.2022)
:::


## Original results

::: {.r-stack}
![](media/bradyetal2018_0.png){fig-align="center" fig-alt="Three panel plot of Maximum Distance Traveled (in meters) as a function of Owner Perceptions of Impulsivity (DIAS) for Adult Study 1, Adult Study 2, and Pup Study. There are no data."}

![](media/bradyetal2018_3_last.png){fig-align="center" .fragment fig-alt="Three panel plot of Maximum Distance Traveled (in meters) as a function of Owner Perceptions of Impulsivity (DIAS) for Adult Study 1, Adult Study 2, and Pup Study. Data show a negative relationship for Adulty Study 1 and 2 but no relationship for Pup Study."}
:::

::: {.source}
[Brady et al. 2018, _Applied Anim Behav Sci_](https://doi.org/10.1016/j.applanim.2018.01.003)
:::


## Our results

::: {.r-stack}
![](media/dog_spatial_results_blank.png){height="550" fig-align="center" fig-alt="Scatterplot of Distance traveled (in meters) as a function of Owner Perception of Impulsivity. There are no data."}

::: {data-id="box1" style="background: #132E53; width: 500px; height: 600px; margin: 10px; position: absolute; top: 50px; left: 515px"}
:::

![](media/dog_spatial_results.png){.fragment height="550" fig-align="center" fig-alt="Scatterplot of Distance traveled (in meters) as a function of Owner Perception of Impulsivity. Data show no relationship."}

::: {data-id="box1" style="background: #132E53; width: 500px; height: 600px; margin: 10px; position: absolute; top: 50px; left: 515px"}
:::

![](media/dog_spatial_results.png){.fragment height="550" fig-align="center" fig-alt="Two scatterplots of Distance traveled (in meters) as a function of Owner Perception of Impulsivity. Data show no relationship in both plots."}

:::

::: {.source}
[Stevens et al. 2022, _Animal Behavior & Cognition_](https://doi.org/10.26451/abc.09.03.02.2022)
:::


## Owners not good at predicting dog impulsivity {.center}

## Study conclusions

::: {.incremental}
* Did not replicate Brady et al., 2018

  - British know their dogs better than Americans
  - Different dog populations (breeds, neuter status)
  - Different owner populations
:::


## Implications for reliability

::: {.incremental}
* Increase sample sizes
* Conduct more replications (within and between labs)
* Increase robustness and reproducibility of analyses
* Post data publicly
* Provide more and consistent info on owners
* Consider populations of owners and dogs

:::


# Aggregating studies

## Impulsivity as a trait in domestic dogs

::: {.callout-tip}
## Research Question

::: {style="font-size: 40px"}
::: {.fragment}
Is impulsivity a behavioral trait in dogs?
:::
::: {.fragment}
* Do dogs' impulsivity levels correlate across tasks?
* Do dogs' impulsivity levels correlate with owner perceptions?
:::
:::
:::

::: {.source}
[Barela et al. 2024, _Journal of Comparative Psychology_](https://doi.org/10.1037/com0000352)
:::


## Systematic review and meta-analysis

* Searched 9,900 records from Scopus, Web of Science, and PsychINFO 
* 13 articles conducted correlations between impulsivity tasks and/or between the Dog Impulsivity Assessment Scale (DIAS) and behavioral tasks

::: {.source}
[Barela et al. 2024, _Journal of Comparative Psychology_](https://doi.org/10.1037/com0000352)
:::


